# Flutter / Dart
.dart_tool/
.packages
.pub-cache/
.pub/
build/
*.iml
.idea/
.vscode/

# Generated narration.
#
# build/audio holds ~136 MB of mp3 for the Hindi corpus plus a segment cache.
# It is DERIVED - tool/generate_audio.py rebuilds it from the content and the
# manifest, and the segment cache means a rebuild is mostly free. Committing it
# would put a binary blob in every clone and every diff, for files that are
# meant to live on R2 and be fetched once by the app.
#
# What IS committed is the recipe: the generator, the corpus rules, and the
# manifest that maps content key -> file + content hash.
#
# MOVED 2026-08-18: the generator now writes to .narration-cache/ instead.
# build/ is Flutter's scratch space and `flutter clean` deletes it - which
# it did, taking 1,660 freshly-paid-for passages and the segment cache.
# This rule is kept only so an older build/audio/ left on someone's disk
# still stays out of git.
build/audio/

# Android
android/.gradle/
android/local.properties
android/app/debug/
android/app/profile/
android/app/release/
*.jks
*.keystore

# iOS
ios/Pods/
ios/.symlinks/
ios/Flutter/Flutter.framework
ios/Flutter/Flutter.podspec
ios/Flutter/ephemeral/
*.mode1v3
*.mode2v3
*.perspectivev3
xcuserdata/

# Credentials - never, under any circumstance.
#
# The Google Cloud org policy blocks service-account key creation, which is why
# this project authenticates with Application Default Credentials instead. ADC
# stores its token outside the repo entirely, so there is nothing here to leak.
# These patterns exist so that a future key, added in a hurry, still cannot be
# committed by accident.
*-key.json
*service-account*.json
*credentials*.json
.env
.env.*

# Supabase local scratch
supabase/.temp/

# Translation worklists - derived, and regenerable in one command.
#
# tool/extract_home_hinglish.py rebuilds the h_batch*.tsv worklists from the
# JSON itself, and the *.done.tsv translations have already been applied INTO
# that JSON, which is the artifact that matters. Keeping 7 MB of intermediate
# TSV would double the repo to preserve a scaffold. The house rules the
# translators worked from are NOT derived and are committed.
tool/hindi/h_batch*.tsv
tool/hindi/d_batch*.tsv
tool/hindi/d_all.tsv
tool/hindi/e_owed*.tsv

# Agent scratch. Subagents were told to use a private subdirectory to stop
# them overwriting each other's temp files, and a relative path put it in the
# REPO rather than the session temp dir. Ignored so it cannot be committed by
# accident; the work itself always lands in lib/ or tool/hindi/.
scratchpad/

# Python bytecode from the tool/ scripts
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# `flutter run` output, redirected to a file. 4.5 MB of it, regenerated on
# every run, and it buries real changes in `git status`.
flutter_run*.log
flutter_*.log

# Generated by the Flutter tool on every build. The file says so itself:
# "This is a generated file; do not edit or check into version control."
.flutter-plugins
.flutter-plugins-dependencies

# Generated narration output and its segment cache.
#
# Lives here rather than under build/ because `flutter clean` deletes build/ and
# .dart_tool/ - and did, taking 1,660 paid-for passages with it. Derived and
# regenerable is only cheap while re-running is free; these cost API quota, so
# between synthesis and upload they are the one artifact worth protecting.
#
# Ignored because the audio's real home is R2, not git.
.narration-cache/

# Generated narration - 66 MB of MP3, and DERIVED rather than authored.
#
# Everything that produces these files is committed and pinned: the text in
# lib/data/weekContent.json, the voice (hi-IN-Chirp3-HD-Callirrhoe), the rate
# and gap settings in tool/generate_audio.py, and a per-passage content hash in
# the manifest. Regenerating the whole library is 114,384 characters - about a
# tenth of the monthly free tier - so losing it costs one command, not data.
#
# Committing it would triple the repo permanently (git cannot drop a blob
# without rewriting history) to protect something that is not at risk. R2 is
# where these are meant to live; this staging directory is local testing.
#
# THE MANIFEST IS COMMITTED on purpose - it is small, it is what
# test/narration_keys_test.dart checks app keys against, and NarrationService
# degrades correctly without the audio: a missing file is caught and falls
# through to the device voice.
#
# ⚠️ If a HUMAN voice artist ever records the flagship passages, those files are
# SOURCE, not derived - no script reproduces a person's voice - and they must
# not be covered by this rule. Decide where they are archived BEFORE paying for
# the session.
assets/narration/hi/*.mp3
assets/narration/hi/*.wav
!assets/narration/hi/.gitkeep

# TTS voice-tuning clips. Scratch from choosing the narration voice
# (hi-IN-Chirp3-HD-Callirrhoe) and the sentence gaps - a decision already made
# and recorded in tool/generate_audio.py. Regenerable from the same script.
voice_samples/

# OS noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Office lock files. Excel drops a hidden "~$Name.xlsx" beside any workbook
# you have open, recording who holds it, and deletes it on a clean close. It
# is invisible in Explorer, so it only ever shows up at `git add` time - and
# the door-content workbooks in docs/content-brief/ get opened often.
~$*

# Content-brief output. Everything in this folder except the README is
# RENDERED, not written: the .xlsx by tools/*_door_content_xlsx.py and
# tools/content_overview_xlsx.py, the .pdf by tools/content_inventory.py and
# tools/parenting_content_brief.py. All of those are tracked, so the output is
# one command away and a binary nobody diffs is pure history weight - 42 MB of
# it for the PDFs alone. The generators are the source; the renders stay local.
#
# The CSVs under research/content-inventory/ are deliberately NOT ignored.
# They are text, they diff, and the README treats them as the source the PDFs
# are merely a layout of - so a bad parse shows up in review instead of hiding
# inside a binary.
#
# Scoped to this folder on purpose: research/competitors/*-critical-reviews.xlsx
# are hand-curated analysis, not output, and stay tracked.
docs/content-brief/*.xlsx
docs/content-brief/*.pdf

# design-sync (Claude Design)
.ds-sync/
ds-bundle/
.design-sync/.cache/
.design-sync/learnings/
.design-sync/node_modules
.design-sync/upload-list.json
.design-sync/chunks.txt
design-kit/node_modules/
design-kit/dist/

# Raw competitor review dumps - source data for research/*, not app content.
# The Play Store CSVs alone run 15-52 MB each; committing them once already
# added ~110 MB to history. Keep them local; the curated *-critical-reviews.xlsx
# analysis files are small and stay tracked.
research/competitors/**/*_reviews_raw.csv

# Door maps and the app flow map. RENDERED, not written: tools/door_map.py,
# tools/skilling_door_map.py and tools/buying_door_map.py lay the section
# content out as one HTML page each and print it to PDF, so the reader can
# see a door whole. Thirty-five files and counting, two per section, and
# every one of them buried real changes in `git status`.
#
# Same rule as docs/content-brief/: the generators are the source and are
# tracked; the renders are one command away and stay local. The *.md design
# prompts beside them are authored and are NOT covered - they diff, and they
# are what the maps are made from.
#
# Matched by name rather than by folder on purpose. docs/BOTTOM-NAV-MAP.* and
# docs/TTC-Master.pdf are already tracked and stay so; a bare docs/*.pdf would
# hide the next such file added by hand.
*-DOOR-MAP.html
*-DOOR-MAP.pdf
docs/APP-FLOW-MAP.html
docs/APP-FLOW-MAP.pdf

# Claude Design prompts. The briefs handed to claude.ai/design to draw a
# screen - input to a design tool, not app work, and the screens they
# produced are what got built. They are kept next to the repo because the
# next prompt is usually written by reading the last one, but they do not
# belong in `git status` and they do not belong in history.
#
# The prompts that were committed BEFORE this rule (docs/*-PROMPT.md and
# docs/_parenting/*.prompt.md) stay where they are; ignoring a path never
# untracks it, and moving them would only churn history. New prompts go in
# this folder. The audits and inventories beside them in docs/ are app
# planning and stay tracked.
docs/design-prompts/

# Mobbin reference screenshots - downloaded because the MCP image URLs expire
# in 30 days. The permanent links live in the docs/*-AUDIT.md index tables.
research/mobbin/**/*.webp
research/mobbin/**/*.jpg
research/mobbin/**/*.png

# Question miner (tools/question_miner/). The embedding model is 66 MB of
# ONNX pulled by fetch_model.py; the SQLite store is rebuilt by every run.
# What IS tracked is the recipe (the tool) and the rendered pool under
# research/questions/ - the CSVs and the workbook are the deliverable.
tools/question_miner/.models/
research/questions/questions.sqlite*
research/questions/*.log

# The onboarding art ships as JPEG (1.3 MB for the set). The generated PNG
# originals beside them are ~19 MB and are kept locally for re-cropping, never
# committed — a download size that matters most in the market this app is for.
assets/onboarding/*.png
